WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:05.642
Ambition: it can spur a man to greatness, but it can also turn a good man to evil.

2
00:00:05.643 --> 00:00:11.983
The story of Macbeth, who became King of Scotland through murder, is the story of one such man.

3
00:00:11.984 --> 00:00:17.847
The story begins on a barren Scottish heath where three witches are making their plans.

4
00:00:17.847 --> 00:00:25.917
"Where shall we three meet again? In thunder, lightning, or in rain?" asked the first witch.

5
00:00:25.918 --> 00:00:37.073
"Before the setting of the sun, when the battle is lost and won, upon the heath to meet Macbeth!" the second witch answered.

6
00:00:37.074 --> 00:00:45.787
The third one chanted, "Fair is foul, and foul is fair," and they disappeared into the filthy fog.

7
00:00:45.788 --> 00:00:52.331
At the same time, at a camp near a battlefield, three men were also talking about Macbeth.

8
00:00:52.331 --> 00:00:58.132
King Duncan of Scotland and Ross, one of his noblemen, were listening to a wounded soldier.

9
00:00:58.132 --> 00:01:03.092
"At first, the battle's outcome was uncertain," the soldier said.

10
00:01:03.092 --> 00:01:10.458
"The rebels swarmed around us, but our two captains, Macbeth and Banquo, beat them back madly.

11
00:01:10.458 --> 00:01:12.606
They were like two lions among kittens!

12
00:01:12.607 --> 00:01:19.268
Then, with his sword, brave Macbeth carved a bloody path through the enemy until he faced the thane of Cawdor.

13
00:01:19.268 --> 00:01:24.489
With two strokes, he cut down the traitorous leader and returned carrying his head."

14
00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:29.752
"The victory is ours," said Ross, after the soldier was carried out.

15
00:01:29.752 --> 00:01:32.505
"Your kinsman, Macbeth, fought bravely."

16
00:01:32.505 --> 00:01:42.456
"Yes," said Duncan, "and what the traitor has lost, Macbeth has gained! I have decided that Macbeth will be the new thane of Cawdor."

17
00:01:42.457 --> 00:01:48.337
As Banquo and Macbeth rode back from the battle, the sunlight was swallowed up by a sudden fog.

18
00:01:48.338 --> 00:01:53.767
"I have never seen such a foul and fair day," Macbeth said. They stopped.

19
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:58.306
Before them, pointing their crooked fingers at Macbeth, stood the three witches.

20
00:01:58.306 --> 00:02:02.029
"What are these withered creatures?" said Banquo.

21
00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:07.314
"Speak, if you can," called Macbeth. "What are you?"

22
00:02:07.315 --> 00:02:12.096
"All hail Macbeth!" said the first witch.

23
00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:18.013
"Hail to thee, Macbeth, Thane of Cawdor," said the second.

24
00:02:18.013 --> 00:02:25.671
With a voice like a crow, the third witch croaked, "Hail Macbeth, who shall be king!"

25
00:02:25.672 --> 00:02:33.555
As Macbeth stood in bewilderment, the witches turned to Banquo and said, "Lesser than Macbeth, and greater.

26
00:02:33.556 --> 00:02:38.457
Not you, but your descendants shall be kings."

27
00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:44.654
Then they vanished, melting into the air. The two men were astounded. What did these words mean?

28
00:02:44.655 --> 00:02:47.885
Had it all been just a trick of their tired minds?

29
00:02:47.886 --> 00:02:51.878
The sun broke through the fog, and Ross rode up to meet them.

30
00:02:51.878 --> 00:02:58.484
"Macbeth, the king has heard the news of your success and thanks you," Ross said.

31
00:02:58.485 --> 00:03:06.118
"He has asked me to name you most worthy Thane of Cawdor. That title is now yours."

32
00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:11.666
A perplexed look came over Macbeth's face. The second witch's words had just come true!

33
00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:16.660
Did this mean the third witch's prophecy would also come true? He would be king!

34
00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:25.913
"Banquo," he whispered, "did you hear? I am the thane of Cawdor! Don't you hope that your children will be kings?"

35
00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:34.020
"It may not be," Banquo whispered back. "Sometimes the devil's servants tempt us with truth to lead us into evil."

36
00:03:34.021 --> 00:03:40.454
Ross spoke up again. He said that King Duncan would dine at Macbeth's castle to honor him in person.

37
00:03:40.454 --> 00:03:47.326
As Macbeth headed home, thoughts rushed through his mind. "I shall be king!" he repeated to himself.

38
00:03:47.326 --> 00:03:51.628
But how could this happen while King Duncan lived? It would take murder.

39
00:03:51.628 --> 00:03:57.675
No, that was too horrible! Luck had made him the thane of Cawdor; luck would make him king.

40
00:03:57.675 --> 00:04:01.898
Macbeth arrived at his castle and told his wife everything that had happened.

41
00:04:01.898 --> 00:04:11.754
Lady Macbeth was elated. "My dearest partner in greatness," she told him, "you shall have what the witches promised."

42
00:04:11.754 --> 00:04:15.819
Her husband went off to remove his armor and clean off the grime of the battle.

43
00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:19.041
Lady Macbeth stood alone in the great hall, thinking.

44
00:04:19.041 --> 00:04:25.752
"My husband has ambition," she said to herself, "but not the villainy to go with it.

45
00:04:25.753 --> 00:04:31.048
He is too full of human kindness to take the quickest path to greatness.

46
00:04:31.055 --> 00:04:35.887
With my tongue, I'll pour unholy courage into his ear!"

47
00:04:35.897 --> 00:04:43.589
Just then a trumpet sounded to signal that King Duncan had arrived. He was accompanied by his two sons and many noblemen.

48
00:04:43.589 --> 00:04:47.103
Lady Macbeth heard the servants running to open the gate.

49
00:04:47.104 --> 00:04:53.326
Night was falling. She went to a window and looked up at the dark clouds, her mind on murder.

50
00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:59.897
"Come, dark night," she called. "Make my blood run thick and cold!"

51
00:04:59.897 --> 00:05:06.835
Macbeth entered the hall and Lady Macbeth turned from the window. "The king has arrived," she said.

52
00:05:06.835 --> 00:05:11.636
"Good," said Macbeth, "He will sleep here tonight."

53
00:05:11.636 --> 00:05:15.308
"Ah, the future is ours!" she said.

54
00:05:15.309 --> 00:05:22.875
As they walked to the courtyard, Lady Macbeth explained to her husband how easy it would be to murder Duncan while he slept.

55
00:05:22.875 --> 00:05:27.040
"He shall not see tomorrow's sun," she said.

56
00:05:27.041 --> 00:05:32.254
Macbeth thought again of the witches' promise, looked at his wife, and agreed.

57
00:05:32.255 --> 00:05:35.595
King Duncan smiled broadly as they approached.

58
00:05:35.596 --> 00:05:39.454
"Your castle has a sweet and pleasant air," he said.

59
00:05:39.454 --> 00:05:48.913
He hugged Macbeth and turned to face the gathered noblemen. Banquo was there and Ross, as well as the king's sons, Malcolm and Donaldbain.

60
00:05:48.914 --> 00:05:54.014
In a loud voice for all to hear, Duncan thanked Macbeth for his bravery and service.

61
00:05:54.015 --> 00:05:59.112
Then the king took Lady Macbeth's arm and chatted pleasantly with her as they walked ahead.

62
00:05:59.112 --> 00:06:05.771
Macbeth walked by himself. "If it were done, it should be done quickly," he said to himself.

63
00:06:05.771 --> 00:06:14.620
"Oh, my thoughts are evil . . . but with one blow─success! And success would end these troubled thoughts."

64
00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:20.350
In the hall, Macbeth saw his wife and the king sitting together and smiling, and his thoughts changed.

65
00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:28.635
"I am Duncan's kinsman and his host. I should be protecting him from murder, not planning to use the knife myself!"

66
00:06:28.635 --> 00:06:34.183
After the feast, Macbeth told his wife, "This business will go no further."

67
00:06:34.183 --> 00:06:37.604
"Has your courage fallen asleep?" she asked.

68
00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:42.403
"Quiet, woman! I dare to do anything that makes a man a man!"

69
00:06:42.405 --> 00:06:47.794
"Then be more than a man, be king!" said Lady Macbeth.

70
00:06:47.795 --> 00:06:53.626
She explained how, during dinner, she had made sure that Duncan's two servants drank too much wine.

71
00:06:53.627 --> 00:06:57.269
They would sleep heavily, leaving Duncan unguarded.

72
00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:03.158
It would be easy to kill Duncan and then cover the servants with his blood in order to frame them for the murder.

73
00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:13.253
"My wife!" Macbeth said with admiration. "I swear, there's nothing soft in you! It is settled. Let's attempt this terrible task."

74
00:07:13.254 --> 00:07:19.936
When all the candles were out and the castle was dark, Macbeth crept quietly toward the room where Duncan slept.

75
00:07:19.937 --> 00:07:24.547
He turned a corner and stopped. Something seemed to float in the hallway ahead of him.

76
00:07:24.548 --> 00:07:28.433
"Is this a dagger which I see before me?" he said.

77
00:07:28.434 --> 00:07:36.246
But when he reached out to grab it, his hand passed right through it. Slowly, the faintly glowing dagger led him forward.

78
00:07:36.247 --> 00:07:45.381
"It is nothing but a dagger of the mind," he told himself. "But look! It has the exact form of the dagger I've pulled from my belt.

79
00:07:45.382 --> 00:07:50.762
And now blood is dripping from its ghostly blade." He shook his head.

80
00:07:50.763 --> 00:07:54.175
"It is nothing─just a figment of my imagination."

81
00:07:54.176 --> 00:08:02.112
He looked down. The dagger had led him past Duncan's sleeping servants, and there before him was the king, lying peacefully in bed.

82
00:08:02.113 --> 00:08:12.442
A bell began to sound in the distance. "Hear it not, Duncan," Macbeth whispered, "for this bell summons you either to heaven or to hell."

83
00:08:12.443 --> 00:08:17.250
And before the bell could wake the sleeping king, Macbeth plunged his dagger down.

